<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 4.3 Partial Derivatives.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  ch-14-3.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id here is prefixed
  with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: all three are three-dimensional plots of a surface with traces
  and tangent lines on it, which set the geometry up rather than answering
  any exercise, so they are reproduced in full, as in the lecture notes.
  The reader is asked to read the slopes off them.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-fmv-partial-derivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Partial Derivatives</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
State the definition of the partial derivatives
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m> and
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> as limits, and interpret each as
the slope of a tangent line to a curve cut from the surface by a vertical
plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute partial derivatives by treating all other variables as
constants, and use the standard notations <m>f_x</m>,
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>, and
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute second partial derivatives, including the mixed partials
<m>f_{xy}</m> and <m>f_{yx}</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Apply the mixed derivative theorem to choose the more convenient order of
differentiation.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
A function of one variable has one derivative, which measures the rate at
which the output changes as the single input changes. A function of two
variables has two inputs, and so it has two rates of change: one obtained
by moving in the <m>x</m>-direction and one by moving in the
<m>y</m>-direction. These are the partial derivatives, and each is an
ordinary one-variable derivative in disguise, taken while the other
variable is held fixed.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-pd-definition">
<title>Definition of Partial Derivatives</title>

<p>
Consider the point <m>P(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> on the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m>, and the two curves through it drawn in
<xref ref="fig-skel-pd-two-tangents"/>: one cut by the vertical plane
<m>y = y_0</m>, the other by <m>x = x_0</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-two-tangents">
<caption>
The two curves through <m>P</m> cut from the surface by the vertical
planes <m>y = y_0</m> and <m>x = x_0</m>, and their tangent lines at
<m>P</m>. The slope of the blue line is
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m> and the slope of the dark red line
is <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="86%">
<description>
<p>
A grey surface with two curves drawn on it through a magenta point P. One
curve, in green, is the trace in the plane y equals y nought and has a
blue tangent line at P. The other, in amber, is the trace in the plane x
equals x nought and has a dark red tangent line at P.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=12cm, height=9cm,
  view={-35}{20},
  xmin=-2.3, xmax=2.3, ymin=-2.3, ymax=2.3, zmin=0, zmax=4.4,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
]
% the surface
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, draw=gray!55, fill=gray!30,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.4,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-2:2, samples=15]
  {3 - 0.25*x^2 - 0.45*y^2};
% trace in the plane y = y0 (green)
\addplot3[very thick, green!50!black, domain=-2:2, samples=33]
  ({x},{0.5},{2.8875 - 0.25*x^2});
% trace in the plane x = x0 (amber)
\addplot3[very thick, orange, domain=-2:2, samples=33]
  ({0.5},{x},{2.9375 - 0.45*x^2});
% tangent line in y = y0, slope df/dx = -0.25
\addplot3[very thick, cyan!60!black, domain=-1.6:1.6, samples=2]
  ({0.5+x},{0.5},{2.825 - 0.25*x});
% tangent line in x = x0, slope df/dy = -0.45
\addplot3[very thick, purple!60!black, domain=-1.6:1.6, samples=2]
  ({0.5},{0.5+x},{2.825 - 0.45*x});
% the point P
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=magenta]
  coordinates {(0.5,0.5,2.825)};
\node[magenta, anchor=west] at (axis cs:0.62,0.55,2.72)
  {$P(x_0,y_0,f(x_0,y_0))$};
% labels with leaders
\node[cyan!60!black, align=center, anchor=south]
  at (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  {Tangent line at $P$\\ in the $y=y_0$ plane};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  -- (axis cs:-0.9,0.5,3.175);
\node[purple!60!black, align=center, anchor=west]
  at (axis description cs:1.0,0.62)
  {Tangent line at $P$\\ in the $x=x_0$ plane};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.62)
  -- (axis cs:0.5,1.7,2.285);
\node[green!50!black, anchor=east] at (axis description cs:0.0,0.80)
  {$z=f(x,y_0)$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.0,0.80)
  -- (axis cs:-1.55,0.5,2.287);
\node[orange, anchor=east] at (axis description cs:0.02,0.36)
  {$z=f(x_0,y)$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.02,0.36)
  -- (axis cs:0.5,-1.5,1.925);
\node[gray!30!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.0,0.20)
  {$z=f(x,y)$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.20)
  -- (axis cs:1.75,1.6,1.08);
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Write down the limit definition of each of the two slopes. (Both are the
ordinary difference quotient you already know, with one input held still:
for <m>\partial f/\partial x</m>, freeze <m>y</m> at <m>y_0</m> and give
<m>x_0</m> an increment <m>h</m>. Say which curve of the figure each one
is the slope of, and which variable is doing the moving.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {$\left.\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {$\left.\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the limit definition of each of the two partial derivatives.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-pd-definition">
<title>The Partial Derivatives</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-pd-def-x" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{f(x_0+h,\, y_0) - f(x_0,y_0)}{h},
</md>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-pd-def-y" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{f(x_0,\, y_0+h) - f(x_0,y_0)}{h}.
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
In practice we never evaluate those limits. Say what we do instead.
(Compare <xref ref="eq-skel-pd-def-x"/> with the one-variable definition:
if <m>y</m> never changes, then <m>f(x,y_0)</m> is a function of <m>x</m>
alone. State the resulting rule for each variable in a single sentence.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {To find $f_x$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {To find $f_y$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, for the practical rule for computing each partial derivative.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-pd-as-ordinary">
<title>Partial Derivatives as Ordinary Derivatives</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-pd-as-ordinary-x" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \left.\frac{d}{dx} f(x,y_0)\right|_{x=x_0},
</md>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-pd-as-ordinary-y" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \left.\frac{d}{dy} f(x_0,y)\right|_{y=y_0}.
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Here are some common notations for the partial derivative with respect to
<m>x</m>:
<md>
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
\qquad
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0,y_0)
\qquad
f_x(x_0,y_0).
</md>
Note that similar notation can be used for the partial derivative with
respect to any variable such as <m>y</m> and <m>z</m>.
</p>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-pd-examples">
<title>Examples</title>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-pd-paraboloid">
<title>Partial Derivatives of <m>1-x^2-y^2</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> and evaluate the
following partial derivatives at the point
<m>(x_0,y_0) = (1/2, 1/2)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Hold the other variable constant, so its term differentiates to zero.
This is the surface of <xref ref="subsec-skel-fsv-functions-two-variables"/>;
after computing, say what the two numbers say about the surface at that
point, and why symmetry made them equal.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {A. $f_x =$ \qquad so $f_x(1/2,1/2) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {B. $f_y =$ \qquad so $f_y(1/2,1/2) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {What they say about the surface:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for each partial derivative and its value, and for their geometric meaning.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-pd-quotient">
<title>A Quotient with a Trigonometric Term</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{2y}{y + \cos x}</m> and evaluate
the following partial derivatives at the point
<m>(x_0,y_0) = (0,0)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(For <m>f_x</m> the numerator <m>2y</m> is a constant, so only the
reciprocal needs differentiating; for <m>f_y</m> the whole thing is a
quotient in <m>y</m>. Watch that <m>\cos x</m> is a constant in the
second case but not the first.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {A. $f_x =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {\phantom{A. }$f_x(0,0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {B. $f_y =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {\phantom{B. }$f_y(0,0) =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for each partial derivative and then its value at the origin.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-pd-elliptic">
<title>Partial Derivatives as Slopes on a Surface</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the elliptic paraboloid <m>z = f(x,y) = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m>.
Evaluate <m>f_x(1,1)</m> and <m>f_y(1,1)</m> and explain what these
numbers represent.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compute both partials as before. Then, for each, substitute the fixed
value to get the equation of the trace curve in that vertical plane, and
say which curve of <xref ref="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-x"/> and
<xref ref="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-y"/> it is. Compare the two slopes: one
tangent line is steeper, and the coefficients in <m>f</m> say why.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {$f_x(1,1) =$ \qquad trace in $y=1$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {$f_y(1,1) =$ \qquad trace in $x=1$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {What each number represents:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Which is steeper, and why:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for each partial derivative with its trace curve, for the meaning of the numbers, and for the comparison of the two slopes.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-x">
<caption>
The trace <m>C_1</m> cut from <m>z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> by the plane
<m>y = 1</m> is the parabola <m>z = 2 - x^2</m>. Its tangent line at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> has slope <m>f_x(1,1) = -2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
A tall dome shaped surface cut by the vertical plane y equals one. The
curve of intersection is drawn in dark blue and labelled C one, and a
magenta tangent line touches it at the point 1, 1, 1.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=10cm, height=9cm,
  view={-40}{16},
  xmin=-2.1, xmax=2.1, ymin=-1.6, ymax=1.6, zmin=0, zmax=4.4,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
]
% the surface z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2 (only where z >= 0)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=cyan!25, draw=cyan!55!black,
          fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-1.41:1.41, samples=15, samples y=13]
  {4 - x^2 - 2*y^2};
% the cutting plane y = 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, domain=-2:2, y domain=0:3.6,
          fill=cyan!45, fill opacity=0.22, draw=cyan!50!black,
          draw opacity=0.35]
  ({x},{1},{y});
% the trace C1: z = 2 - x^2 in the plane y = 1
\addplot3[very thick, blue!55!black, domain=-1.41:1.41, samples=33]
  ({x},{1},{2 - x^2});
% tangent line at (1,1,1), slope -2 in the x-direction
\addplot3[very thick, magenta, domain=-0.85:0.85, samples=2]
  ({1+x},{1},{1 - 2*x});
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8, color=magenta]
  coordinates {(1,1,1)};
\node[magenta, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.15,1,0.75) {$(1,1,1)$};
\node[blue!55!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.0,0.66) {$C_1$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.66)
  -- (axis cs:-1.15,1,0.677);
\node[cyan!50!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.0,0.46) {$y=1$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.46)
  -- (axis cs:-1.7,1,0.5);
\node[cyan!55!black, anchor=south] at (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  {$z=4-x^2-2y^2$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  -- (axis cs:-0.6,-0.7,2.66);
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-y">
<caption>
The trace <m>C_2</m> cut from <m>z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> by the plane
<m>x = 1</m> is the parabola <m>z = 3 - 2y^2</m>. Its tangent line at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> has slope <m>f_y(1,1) = -4</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
The same dome shaped surface, now cut by the vertical plane x equals one.
The curve of intersection is drawn in dark blue and labelled C two, and a
magenta tangent line touches it at the point 1, 1, 1. This tangent line
is noticeably steeper than the one in the previous figure.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
